    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-72382",
      "knowledge_point_id": "b89c50f3-2181-5b61-96cb-386ef88d2035",
      "knowledge_point": "Integrating an Expression Involving a Power Function Multiplied by Its Derivative",
      "problem": "Calculate $\\int \\frac{5{x}^{4}}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{5}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$",
      "worked_solution": "We note that $5{x}^{4}$ is the derivative of the quantity in the radical. So we make a substitution\n\n$$\nu=1+{x}^{5}.\n$$\n\nIts derivative is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =5{x}^{4}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=5{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand rewriting our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5{x}^{4}}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{5}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{5}}}\\,5{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{{u}^{1/2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\int {u}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now solve the integral using the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\frac{{u}^{1/2}}{(\\frac{1}{2})}+C \\\\  & =2{u}^{1/2}+C \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{u}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we substitute $u=1+{x}^{5}$ back in the expression. So the final result is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5{x}^{4}}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{5}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\sqrt{1+{x}^{5}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
