    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-2189",
      "knowledge_point_id": "b89c50f3-2181-5b61-96cb-386ef88d2035",
      "knowledge_point": "Integrating an Expression Involving a Power Function Multiplied by Its Derivative",
      "problem": "Calculate $\\int 3{x}^{2}({x}^{3}+4)^{5}\\,\\text{d}x.$",
      "worked_solution": "We note that $3{x}^{2}$ is the derivative of the quantity in the parentheses. So we make a substitution\n\n$$\nu={x}^{3}+4.\n$$\n\nIts derivative is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =3{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand rewriting our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 3{x}^{2}({x}^{3}+4)^{5}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int ({x}^{3}+4)^{5}\\,3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {u}^{5}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now solve the integral using the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\int {u}^{5}\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{{u}^{6}}{6}+C\n$$\n\nLastly, we substitute $u={x}^{3}+4$ back in the expression. So the final result is\n\n$$\n\\int 3{x}^{2}({x}^{3}+4)^{5}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{({x}^{3}+4)^{6}}{6}+C.\n$$",
