      "math_academy_id": "q-113581",
      "knowledge_point_id": "b89c50f3-2181-5b61-96cb-386ef88d2035",
      "knowledge_point": "Integrating an Expression Involving a Power Function Multiplied by Its Derivative",
      "problem": "Calculate $\\int 12{x}^{5}\\sqrt{2{x}^{6}-3}\\,\\text{d}x.$",
      "worked_solution": "We note that $12{x}^{5}$ is the derivative of the quantity in the square root. So we make a substitution\n\n$$\nu=2{x}^{6}-3.\n$$\n\nIts derivative is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =12{x}^{5}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=12{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand rewriting our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 12{x}^{5}\\sqrt{2{x}^{6}-3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\sqrt{2{x}^{6}-3}\\cdot 12{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\int {u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now solve the integral using the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\frac{{u}^{3/2}}{(\\frac{3}{2})}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2{u}^{3/2}}{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we substitute $u=2{x}^{6}-3$ back in the expression. So the final result is\n\n$$\n\\int 12{x}^{5}\\sqrt{2{x}^{6}-3}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{2(2{x}^{6}-3)^{3/2}}{3}+C.\n$$",
