    {
      "math_academy_id": "e-452",
      "knowledge_point_id": "b89c50f3-2181-5b61-96cb-386ef88d2035",
      "knowledge_point": "Integrating an Expression Involving a Power Function Multiplied by Its Derivative",
      "problem": "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\int 6x^2(2x^3+3)^6\\,\\textrm{d}x .$",
      "worked_solution": "We note that $6x^2$ is the derivative of the quantity in the parentheses. So we make a substitution\n\n$$\nu=2x^3+3.\n$$\n\nIts derivative is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =6{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=6{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand rewriting our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 6{x}^{2}(2{x}^{3}+3)^{6}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (2{x}^{3}+3)^{6}\\,6{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {u}^{6}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now solve the integral using the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}^{6}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\frac{{u}^{7}}{7}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we substitute $u=2x^3+3$ back in the expression. So the final result is\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int 6x^2(2x^3+3)^6\\,\\textrm{d}x =\\dfrac{(2x^3+3)^7}{7}+C.\n$$",
      "difficulty": null,
      "answer_fields": [],
      "missing_source_fields": [
        "difficulty",
        "answer_fields"
      ]
    },
    {
      "math_academy_id": "e-453",
      "knowledge_point_id": "8fe2a279-0b92-561e-b17d-b902f43725ac",
      "knowledge_point": "Integrating an Expression Involving a Power Function Multiplied by a Constant Multiple of Its Derivative",
      "problem": "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\int x\\left(1+2x^2\\right)^6\\,\\textrm{d}x .$",
      "worked_solution": "The $x$ that's outside the parentheses isn't quite the derivative of $(1+2x^2).$ The derivative of $(1+2x^2)$ is $4x,$ so we're out by a constant factor of $4$. However, since we're out only by a constant factor, substitution will still work!\n\nSo we make the substitution\n\n$$\nu=1+2x^2.\n$$\n\nIts derivative is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =4x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u=x\\,\\text{d}x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we can now write our integral in terms of $u,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{(1+2{x}^{2})}^{6}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {(1+2{x}^{2})}^{6}\\cdot x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {u}^{6}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int {u}^{6}\\text{d}u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve the integral in terms of $u$ and then write the final result in terms of $x.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}\\int {u}^{6}\\text{d}u & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{7}}{7}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{28}{u}^{7}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{28}{(1+2{x}^{2})}^{7}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "difficulty": null,
      "answer_fields": [],
      "missing_source_fields": [
        "difficulty",
        "answer_fields"
      ]
    },
    {
      "math_academy_id": "e-454",
      "knowledge_point_id": "18ce1807-3a09-5ebd-b3cd-a91db8d2b4a8",
      "knowledge_point": "Integrating a Rational Function Using Natural Logarithms",
      "problem": "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{x^2}{x^3+1}\\,\\textrm d x.$",
      "worked_solution": "Let $u=x^3+1.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 3x^2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac 1 3 \\textrm d u= x^2 \\, \\textrm d x,\n$$\n\nand we can solve the resulting integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+1}\\cdot {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{3}+1|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "difficulty": null,
      "answer_fields": [],
      "missing_source_fields": [
        "difficulty",
        "answer_fields"
      ]
    },
    {
      "math_academy_id": "e-6149",
      "knowledge_point_id": "03e934c8-da65-5615-9717-a50eee3577fd",
      "knowledge_point": "Integrating Other Fractional Expressions",
      "problem": "$\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{x^2}{\\sqrt{1-x^3}} \\, \\textrm{d}x=$",
      "worked_solution": "Let $u = 1 - x^3.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = -3x^2 \\quad \\Longrightarrow\\quad -\\dfrac{1}{3} \\textrm d u= x^2 \\, \\textrm d x,\n$$\n\nand we can solve the resulting integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{3}}}\\cdot {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3})\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}(2\\sqrt{u})+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{u}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{1-{x}^{3}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "difficulty": null,
      "answer_fields": [],
      "missing_source_fields": [
        "difficulty",
        "answer_fields"
      ]
    }
  ]
